00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.230
So what is Google
Compute Engine?

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:25.130
Compute Engine is an intuitive,
and flexible, simple at scale,

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.700
and secured compute service
that lets you create and run

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.840
your applications on Google
Cloud's global infrastructure.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.820
Let's talk flexibility.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.160
There are six
different VM families--

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.020
three within the
general purpose,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.430
and three that are optimized
for specific types of workloads.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.490
General purpose VMs are
ideal for a wide variety

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.630
of applications like web
serving, databases, and image

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.690
processing, and come
in flavors optimized

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.800
for either cost or a balance
of price and performance.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.670
Workload optimized VMs
include compute, memory,

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.680
and accelerator
optimized options

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.930
for your most
demanding applications,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:10.280
such as SAP Hana and high
performance computing.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.500
You can achieve
even greater savings

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.440
through taking
advantage of Spot VMs.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.110
Spot VMs are affordable
compute instances

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.220
suitable for batch jobs and
fault tolerant workloads.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.090
Google Cloud Spot VMs guarantee
higher minimum savings

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.150
and more predictability in
pricing for spot instances

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.680
compared to other
cloud providers.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.470
Let's talk security.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.510
Google Cloud and
Compute Engine are

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.200
designed to be secure by
default. For enhanced security,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.600
you can leverage Shielded VMs.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.770
Shielded VM features
include verifiable integrity

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.200
with secure and measured boot.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.510
Secure boot helps
prevent malicious code

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.600
from being loaded early
in the boot sequence.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.600
Measured boot ensures the
integrity of the bootloader

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.850
and kernel, and boot
drivers to guard

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.630
against malicious
modifications to the VM.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:06.500
vTPM Exfiltration Resistance--
you can validate your guest VM

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.610
pre-boot and boot integrity
using vTPM technology, which

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.790
is compatible with trusted
computing group TPM 2.0

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.470
specifications.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.620
Trusted UEFI firmware--
trusted firmware

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.310
is based on the Unified
Extensible Firmware Interface,

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:27.020
or UEFI 2.3.1, which
replaces legacy BIOS

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.160
subsystems and enables UEFI
secure boot capability.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.580
Tamper evident
attestations-- gain insight

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.520
into the integrity
state of Shielded VMs

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.730
with tamper evident attestation
claims available in Cloud

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.130
Logging and Cloud Monitoring.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.680
These integrity measurements
help identify changes

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.250
from the healthy baseline of
your VM and current runtime

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.210
state.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.370
Live migration and patching--

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.530
keep your virtual
machines instances

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.600
running even when a host
system event occurs,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.230
such as software or
hardware updates.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.355
You can define IM
policies and permissions,

00:04:02.355 --> 00:04:04.670
and you can set these
policies and permissions

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.470
that constrain all
new Compute Engine

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.830
instances to use
Shielded VM disk images

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.000
and have vTPM and integrity
monitoring options enabled.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.519
You can shield existing
VM images, too.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:19.190
Transform your existing
VMs into Shielded VMs

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.310
that run on Google Cloud,
bringing verifiable integrity

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.210
and exfiltration resistance
to your existing images.

